Volume of an arbitrary spherical bubble, $V_{sphere} = \frac{4}{3}\pi {r_s}^3$. The hemisphere is $\frac{1}{2}$ of a sphere, so its volume is half of the sphere's volume: \
\begin{align*}
V_{hemisphere} &= \frac{1}{2} \times \frac{4}{3}\pi {r_s}^3\\
&= \frac{2}{3}\pi {r_s}^3\\
\end{align*}

The hemisphere is the surface of the bubble plus the area of the flat surface above it, which forms a circle. The radius of this circle is $r=2\sqrt[3]{2}$ and the area is $A=4\pi \left(\sqrt[3]{2}\right)^2$. The remaining bubble surface after the bubble lands is in the shape of a hemisphere with the hemisphere already accounting for a part of the bubble itself, as shown in figure 1. This means we have to calculate the remaining surface of the rest of the sphere to this hemisphere, which is:
\begin{align*}
\text{Surface area of the remaining part} &= \text{Area of the sphere} - \text{Surface area of the hemisphere}\\
&= 4\pi {r_s}^2 - 2\pi {r_s}^2\\
&= 2\pi {r_s}^2\\
\end{align*}
So, the total surface of the rest of the sphere is half the original surface area of the sphere plus the remaining surface of the rest of the sphere. If the original radius of the bubble is $r_s$, then the rest of the surface is where $$\frac{2}{3}\pi {r_s}^3=\frac{1}{2} \times 4\pi {r_s}^2 + 2\pi {r_s}^2$$
\begin{align*}
\frac{2}{3}\pi {r_s}^3 &= 2\pi {r_s}^2 + 2\pi {r_s}^2\\
\frac{2}{3}\pi {r_s}^3 &= 4\pi {r_s}^2\\
{r_s}^3 &= 6{r_s}^2\\
{r_s}^3 - 6{r_s}^2 &= 0\\
{r_s}^2({r_s} - 6) &= 0\\
{r_s} &= 6 \implies \text{ raiaq}
\end{align*}


